---
import InfoLayout from '../layouts/InfoLayout.astro';
import InfoSection from '../components/InfoSection.astro';
import SpecTable from '../components/SpecTable.astro';
---

<InfoLayout
  path="/turnaround-time/"
  title="Turnaround Time | Packaging Production Lead Times"
  description="What affects packaging lead times, which stages you control, and how we confirm a production window in writing."
  h1="Turnaround Time"
  eyebrow="Ordering"
  lead="We confirm a production window in writing with every quote rather than publishing a fixed figure, because the honest answer depends on your specification and on how quickly approvals come back."
  ogTitle="Packaging Lead Times and What Affects Them"
  ogDescription="The stages that make up a packaging lead time, which you control, and how to compress a schedule safely."
  points={['Confirmed per order', 'Stages you control', 'Rush options where possible']}
  ctaHeading="Working to a fixed launch date?"
  ctaBody="Tell us the date at enquiry stage rather than at order stage. It changes which print process and finishing we would recommend, and there is usually a route that works."
>
  <InfoSection
    id="why-no-fixed-number"
    heading="Why we do not publish a single lead time"
    intro="A number on a website that does not apply to your job is worse than no number at all."
  >
    <p class="measure">
      A digitally printed carton with a varnish finish moves through production
      quickly. The same carton in offset litho with foil stamping, spot UV and a
      die-cut insert involves several additional passes, and a rigid box adds hand
      assembly on top. Those are genuinely different schedules.
    </p>
    <p class="measure">
      More importantly, a large part of most lead times sits on the customer side:
      die line approval, artwork supply and proof sign-off. A job where proofs come
      back the same day finishes noticeably sooner than an identical job where they
      take a fortnight, and no supplier can quote around that.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      <strong>What we do commit to:</strong> a written production window with your
      quote, and an updated one if the specification changes. If a date slips on our
      side you will hear it from us rather than finding out later.
    </p>
  </InfoSection>

  <InfoSection
    id="stages"
    heading="What makes up a lead time"
    intro="Six stages. Three of them are yours, which is useful to know when a schedule is tight."
  >
    <SpecTable
      caption="Lead time stages and who controls each."
      columns={['Stage', 'Controlled by', 'What speeds it up']}
      rows={[
        ['Quoting', 'Us', 'Complete dimensions and quantity in the first enquiry'],
        ['Die line issue', 'Us', 'A physical container sample rather than a drawing'],
        ['Die line approval', 'You', 'Testing the structural sample promptly with real product'],
        ['Artwork supply', 'You', 'Building to the die line from the start; regulated copy pre-checked'],
        ['Proof approval', 'You', 'One decision-maker rather than a committee, especially across variants'],
        ['Production', 'Us', 'Fewer finishing passes; digital rather than litho at low volume'],
      ]}
    />
  </InfoSection>

  <InfoSection
    id="what-adds-time"
    heading="What adds time to production"
    intro="Each of these is a separate pass or a separate tool. None is unreasonable, but they accumulate."
  >
    <ul class="bullets">
      <li><strong>Offset litho plates</strong> — plate making adds a step that digital printing does not have.</li>
      <li><strong>Lamination</strong> — a converting pass after printing, plus curing time.</li>
      <li><strong>Foil stamping and embossing</strong> — a metal die has to be made before the pass can run.</li>
      <li><strong>Spot UV</strong> — a screen or plate plus an additional pass.</li>
      <li><strong>Window patching</strong> — an extra machine pass, and film has to be in stock.</li>
      <li><strong>Inserts</strong> — a separate die and often a separate production run.</li>
      <li><strong>Rigid construction</strong> — wrapping, corner finishing and magnet setting are partly manual.</li>
      <li><strong>New tube diameter</strong> — rolled paper tubes are tooled per diameter.</li>
      <li><strong>Multiple SKUs</strong> — more artwork to approve and more dies to make, though far quicker than sequential orders.</li>
    </ul>
  </InfoSection>

  <InfoSection
    id="compressing"
    heading="How to compress a schedule safely"
    intro="Five things genuinely help. Two commonly suggested shortcuts are not worth taking."
  >
    <p class="measure"><strong>Worth doing:</strong></p>
    <ul class="bullets">
      <li>Post a container sample with the enquiry, so the die line is drawn once and correctly.</li>
      <li>Have artwork built to the die line before the quote is even accepted.</li>
      <li>Get regulated copy reviewed in parallel with structural sampling rather than after it.</li>
      <li>Nominate one person to approve proofs, and give them the authority to do it.</li>
      <li>Simplify the finishing. Dropping one pass often saves more time than any expediting.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="measure"><strong>Not worth doing:</strong></p>
    <ul class="bullets">
      <li>
        Skipping the structural sample. It saves a few days and risks the entire
        run, because a fit problem found after production is unrecoverable.
      </li>
      <li>
        Approving a digital proof on kraft, metallised or holographic stock. On
        those substrates a screen proof does not predict the printed result, which
        is explained on the <a href="/printing-options/">printing options page</a>.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </InfoSection>

  <InfoSection
    id="rush"
    heading="Rush schedules"
    intro="Often possible, and the constraints are worth knowing before you ask."
  >
    <p class="measure">
      Where a launch date is fixed, we will look at what can be brought forward.
      Usually that means recommending a print process and a finishing specification
      that suit the timeline, rather than expediting an elaborate specification. A
      digitally printed carton with a varnish can be turned around considerably
      faster than a foiled litho job, and on a tight deadline that trade is often
      the right one.
    </p>
    <p class="measure">
      What cannot be compressed is your approval time and the physical curing of
      laminates and inks. Tell us the date early and we will tell you plainly
      whether it is achievable and what would have to change.
    </p>
  </InfoSection>

  <InfoSection
    id="delivery"
    heading="Transit time is separate"
    intro="Production time and delivery time are two different things, and they are quoted separately."
  >
    <p class="measure">
      A production window ends when the goods leave, not when they arrive. Transit
      depends on the destination, the freight method and the volume of the
      consignment, and rolled tubes and rigid boxes are bulky enough that volume
      rather than weight drives the cost. How freight is arranged and quoted is set
      out on the <a href="/shipping-information/">delivery page</a>.
    </p>
  </InfoSection>
</InfoLayout>
